One way to resolve this issue is by using CSS to set the height of the scrolling <code>div</code> and then adding the <code>overflow-y: scroll;</code> property to enable vertical scrolling. This way, the <code>div</code> will be the only element with scrolling and the default scrolling of the entire page can be disabled using the JS codes you mentioned.

Here's an example CSS code:

<pre><code>#scrolling-div {
  height: 100vh; /* adjust height as needed */
  overflow-y: scroll;
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
}
</code></pre>

And then in your JS code:

<pre><code>document.querySelector('#scrolling-div').addEventListener('touchmove', function (event) {
  event.stopPropagation();
});
</code></pre>

This will allow touch scrolling only within the scrolling <code>div</code> and prevent the default page scrolling.

