The TOML encoder in Rust is used to convert Rust data structures to TOML format and write them to a file. 

To use the encoder, you need to first define your data structures as Rust structs. In your case, you could define a struct for each server with fields for IP address and port number:

<pre><code>struct Server {
    ip: String,
    port: u32,
}
</code></pre>

Then, you would create instances of the struct for each server in your vector:

<pre><code>let servers = vec![
    ("A".to_string(), Server { ip: "192.168.4.1".to_string(), port: 4476 }),
    ...
];
</code></pre>

(Note that I have removed the unnecessary parentheses in your vector type signature.)

Finally, you can use the encoder to write the servers to a TOML file:

<pre><code>let mut file = File::create("servers.toml")?;
let mut encoder = toml::Encoder::new();
let mut table = encoder.table();
for (name, server) in &servers {
    let mut server_table = encoder.table();
    server_table.insert("Ipaddr", &server.ip);
    server_table.insert("Port no", &server.port);
    table.insert(name, server_table);
}
let toml_string = encoder.toml_string().expect("Failed to encode TOML");
write!(file, "{}", toml_string).expect("Failed to write TOML to file");
</code></pre>

This code creates a TOML encoder, iterates over the servers, creates a TOML table for each server, inserts the IP address and port number into the table, and then inserts the server table into the main TOML table using the server name as the key. Finally, it writes the TOML string to the file.

Note that this code assumes that you have added the `toml` crate to your project's dependencies in `Cargo.toml`.

