The three reference classes in Java serve different purposes for managing memory.

1. `SoftReference`: Objects referenced by a `SoftReference` are only cleared out of memory when the JVM runs low on memory. This makes them useful for caching data that can be recalculated later if necessary. When an object is only weakly reachable, it can still be retrieved by a call to `get()`, but its reference status will change to `null` if reclaimed by garbage collection.

2. `WeakReference`: Objects referenced by a `WeakReference` are cleared out of memory as soon as no strong references exist to the object. This makes them useful for registering callbacks or listeners where you want automatic cleanup and you're not concerned if the object suddenly becomes null (null-checking is not required).

3. `PhantomReference`: Objects referenced by a `PhantomReference` are not cleared when the object is no longer reachable; they are only cleared when the `ReferenceQueue` of the `PhantomReference` is polled. These are useful for tracking when objects are truly dead and allowing for cleanup tasks to be performed just before the object is reclaimed.

The reason there is no `SoftHashMap` or `PhantomHashMap` is that these classes would not provide any benefit; the size of the map would not change based on memory pressure, only the entries within the map would be affected by GC. Therefore, it's better to use a regular `HashMap` with `SoftReference` or `WeakReference` values.

In your code, if `ref` has been cleared by GC, then `ref.get()` will return `null`. You should modify your code to check if `ref.get()` is null before using it in any way, such as with an if-statement. For example:

```
WeakReference<String> ref = new WeakReference<String>("Hello!");
String value = ref.get();
if (value != null) {
    System.gc();
    value = ref.get();
    if (value != null) {
        System.out.println(value);
    }
}
```
